<h1>Live — bare ruby_llm</h1>

<p>
  A real model, not a fixture. <code>chat.ask</code> yields chunks,
  <code>Message#stream_from</code> appends each one and seals once, and the
  engine turns the growing buffer into frames. There is no adapter and no agent
  framework in between: that method is the whole integration.
</p>

<p>
  Watch three things. Blocks arrive one at a time and the last one carries the
  caret. A block <strong>seals</strong> once <code>seal_lag</code> blocks follow
  it, and never changes again. The seal takes
  <code>data-ms-streaming</code> off the message, which stops the reveal and
  re-enables the copy and download controls.
</p>

<p>
  <code>ms-repair</code> is live on every record here, with a keyframe every four
  seconds, so the mounted engine's manifest and blocks endpoints are being
  called for real while the page is open. The agent version of this page is at
  <%= link_to "/harness/agent", harness_agent_path %>.
</p>

<%= render "harness/console",
      llm: @llm,
      messages: @messages,
      conversation_id: Harness::ChatsController::CONVERSATION_ID,
      form_url: harness_chat_path,
      default_prompt: "Write a short status report in markdown: a heading, a paragraph with bold and a link, a three-row table, and a fenced ruby code block." %>
